WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.207
This example shows a very common design: a generated surrogate key plus a protected natural identifier.

00:00:06.207 --> 00:00:09.387
PostgreSQL generates patient ID automatically.

00:00:09.387 --> 00:00:12.407
That gives us a stable internal key that can be used in relationships.

00:00:12.407 --> 00:00:19.397
The clinic's medical record number still has business meaning, so it receives a UNIQUE constraint.

00:00:19.397 --> 00:00:21.622
The surrogate key does not make the natural business identifier irrelevant.

00:00:21.622 --> 00:00:25.907
Run the example in the browser.

00:00:25.907 --> 00:00:29.532
Notice that the INSERT does not provide a patient ID because PostgreSQL generates it.

00:00:29.532 --> 00:00:32.447
Then inspect the returned row and the table structure.

00:00:32.447 --> 00:00:35.572
If you copy this example into pgAdmin, focus on the two different roles:

00:00:35.572 --> 00:00:40.277
patient ID is the internal row identity, while MRN is protected business identity.

00:00:40.277 --> 00:00:42.782
Keys are not merely technical identifiers.

00:00:42.782 --> 00:00:48.522
A primary key protects row identity, a unique constraint protects a business rule,

00:00:48.522 --> 00:00:50.857
and a foreign key protects a relationship.

00:00:50.857 --> 00:00:53.267
In production systems, surrogate keys are often convenient,

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:58.640
but important natural identifiers may still need their own uniqueness rule.
